    "title": "Census: how long is the recovery window, and how often is ordered-h3 already satisfied by chance?",
    "description": "Pure instrumentation, no scheduling change. In spur/spur-core/src/simulator/: keep the recovering[node] interval tracking from recovery-completion-race-window but delete the weight multiplier and its config field. Emit in util_stats: recovery_window.{count, mean_events_open, p50, p90, max} measured in scheduler steps between restart and first applied post-restart delivery; ordered_h3.{runs_with_overlap, runs_with_h3} so ordered-h3 rate can be divided by unordered h3Rate; and a cross-tab ordered_h3_by_depth[d] for d in 0..8. Gate behind an existing stats-verbosity flag if one exists, else always-on (counters only). Run one chunk and read the numbers; do not run a scheduling A/B.",
    "category": "feedback",
    "buildsOn": [
      "crash_recovery",
      "util_stats counters"
    ],
    "expectedGain": 5,
    "expectedCost": 1.5,
    "rationale": "The parent's null is uninterpretable without knowing whether the recovery window is measurably open and whether ordered-h3 was already common. If ordered-h3 is already ~0.3 of runs, the entire ordered/unordered framing is dead and several pool hypotheses that assume crash-ordering headroom can be dropped at once. If the window is p90 < 2 steps, the null is an implementation-window problem and a *different* intervention (extend the window, don't reweight into it) is indicated. One cheap chunk buys the branch.",
    "generalityArgument": "Counts only restart events, applied-delivery events, and existing depth buckets; no handler names, no protocol roles, no spec-specific predicates. Emits stats only, changes no scheduling decision, so it cannot bias any protocol.",
